\title{Directory-based coherence scales by replacing broadcasts with point-to-point messages}

\p{A scalable [[GA7N]] organization for systems too large for a shared bus. Each memory address has a \em{home node}; the home node maintains a \em{directory} entry listing which caches currently hold copies and in what state. Coherence transactions are point-to-point messages routed via the directory rather than broadcasts. The directory is itself distributed — each node owns the entries for the addresses in its local DRAM, and a physical address uniquely identifies its home — so there is no central bottleneck.}

\p{Compared to [snoop-based coherence](8C2H), directory protocols trade a level of indirection (3 hops instead of 2 for some transactions) for vastly reduced bandwidth — making them viable at hundreds of cores.}

\figure{
\<html:img>[width]{100\%}[src]{\route-asset{assets/img/directory-write.png}}{}
\figcaption{4-hop directory write protocol.}
}

\p{Three refinements make directory coherence practical. Its metadata is kept from scaling with memory by [sparse directories](QSWW); its multi-hop transactions force the protocol to reason about [transient states and races](Z32G); and workload-specific patterns like [migratory sharing](20SW) let it collapse pairs of transactions into one. See [[HHZO]] for a full worked implementation in Murphi.}
